  "description": "These NSTA layers show the current offshore carbon dioxide storage licences, storage sites and storage complexes on the UK Continental Shelf, each published in WGS84, ED50 and ETRS89. The NSTA is the licensing authority for offshore CO2 storage in the UK, issuing storage and appraisal licences and permits and keeping the carbon storage public register; the layer text explains that captured CO2 from industries such as steel and cement, hydrogen production and power generation is shipped or piped to geological formations deeper than about 800 m. The licence layer held 25 features on 26 September 2026, with fields for licence reference and name, type, status, licensing round, operator and partner organisations, award and start dates, permit approval, and links to licence documents and the public register. Data are served from the NSTA open data hub as an ArcGIS feature service and as CSV, shapefile, GeoJSON, KML and file geodatabase downloads, free under the NSTA Open User Licence. Caveats: layers show current licences only and change as rounds are awarded. For simulation it gives the offshore end points of planned CCS networks, useful to model industrial cluster decarbonisation and CO2 transport and storage capacity. Related catalogue records include the IEA CCUS projects database and the UK regulated installations CO2 emissions data.",
